Treating a myocardial infarction costs $30,000–$100,000; preventing it through statins and antihypertensives costs $200–$500 per year — a 50:1 cost ratio in favour of prevention.
Countries that invest heavily in primary care (Netherlands, UK, Japan) have significantly lower per-capita health spending and better population health than countries that rely on specialist-led treatment (USA).
Employers who fund employee wellness programmes report $3–6 return for every dollar invested through reduced absenteeism and healthcare claims.
